WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.737
This query groups order rows by product.

00:00:02.737 --> 00:00:06.822
The output grain changes from one order row to one product.

00:00:06.822 --> 00:00:10.772
That grain change is important in PostgreSQL and in Spark.

00:00:10.772 --> 00:00:18.002
In Spark, GROUP BY can also create a shuffle because rows with the same product may need to

00:00:18.002 --> 00:00:19.107
be brought together across workers.

00:00:19.107 --> 00:00:24.957
Connect this idea to a lakehouse processing millions of web events across many files.

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:30.597
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:38.097
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:38.097 --> 00:00:44.022
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.022 --> 00:00:50.000
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
